BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Web Design for Entrepreneurs (Newcomer Stream)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260603T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260603T193000
UID:69d95a8ec1cf6f9a75847e24@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:In this\n8-week course\n\, learn how to build a strong web pre
 sence for your business in Toronto\, whether you’re launching something 
 new or already running one. Explore how to create a website using WordPres
 s and understand why it remains a powerful tool\, even in the age of AI we
 bsite builders. While AI can help you get started quickly\, WordPress giv
 es you full control over your content\, updates\, and long-term growth. By
  the end\, you’ll feel more confident managing your own website\, saving
  costs while strengthening your marketing at the same time.\nParticipants 
 should plan to attend all 8 of the following d\nates:\nMay 6\, May 13\,\n
 May 20\nJune 3\, June 17\, June 24\nJuly 8\, July 15\nRegistration is requ
 ired for the full series\n. This program is hosted by the Business\, Scien
 ce and Technology department. Please visit the 4th floor service desk or c
 all (416) 395-5613 for more information.\nThis program is part of the\n"Bu
 ilding your own Website for Small Business" Program Series (Newcomer strea
 m)\n.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this fo
 rm is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries A
 ct\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used
  for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions a
 bout how the library handles your information can be directed to Answerlin
 e staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toront
 o ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, e-Learning Lab\, 2nd Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69d95a8ec1cf6f9a75847e24
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Wellness Recovery Action Plan (WRAP)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260515T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260515T130000
UID:696eca617d180d0d4830c461@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:WRAP is for\nanyone\nlooking to strengthen their well-being—
 whether you’re supporting your mental health\, managing stress\, or simp
 ly wanting to feel more balanced and resilient in daily life. Participant
 s will receive a\nWRAP certificate\nupon program completion.\nIn an 8-wee
 k WRAP group\, individuals explore:\nself-care tools like peer connections
 \, relaxation and mindfulness techniques\, stress reduction strategies\, a
 nd more\nresources for keeping well and feeling better in difficult times.
 \nThis WRAP Group meets\nevery Friday\,\nonce a week for 8 weeks\, March 2
 0-May 15\, 2026\, 10 am - 1 pm\n. There is no session on Friday April 3\, 
 2026 due to Good Friday. Registration closes after week 3\, or when full.
 \nTTC Presto tickets will be provided.\nSnacks and refreshments are provid
 ed for all participants. Ingredient lists will be available at the progra
 m.\nThis program is in collaboration with\nGerstein Crisis Centre's WRAP i
 nitiative\n. Gerstein Crisis Centre is solely responsible for the protecti
 on of \npersonal information collected through the program registration \n
 process.
LOCATION:Sanderson\, Basement Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/696eca617d180d0d4830c461
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Dana A. Williams: Toni at Random *NEW DATE*
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260608T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260608T200000
UID:69a5f8bd8089f23c675f5420@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Before Toni Morrison became a Nobel Prize-winning novelist\, s
 he was a groundbreaking editor at Random House who quietly revolutionized 
 American publishing.\nIn her revelatory book\nToni at Random: The Iconic W
 riter's Legendary Editorship\n\, Dr. Dana A. Williams unveils the untold s
 tory of Morrison's sixteen years behind the editorial desk. From 1967 to 1
 983\, Morrison championed over fifty works\, publishing Angela Davis\, Muh
 ammad Ali\, Toni Cade Bambara\, Gayl Jones\, and many others. She pushed b
 oundaries\, elevated emerging voices\, and insisted that Black literature 
 be taken seriously at a time when mainstream publishing largely ignored or
  tokenized Black writers.\nWhat made Morrison's editorial vision so revolu
 tionary? She didn't just recognize great writing\, she shaped how books we
 re marketed\, fought for authentic representation\, and mentored a generat
 ion of authors in the wake of the Black Arts Movement.\nDr. Dana A. Willia
 ms shares her book Toni at Random with host Garvia Bailey and discusses th
 e editorial genius that stands alongside Toni Morrison's celebrated fictio
 n.\nTicket registration for this event is required:\nFree tickets for this
  event are available to book via Eventbrite.\nAbout this event's guests:\n
 Dr. Dana A. Williams\nGarvia Bailey\nRead More:\nToni at Random: The Iconi
 c Writer's Legendary Editorialship\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nBram
  and Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2n
 d floor\nTime:\nDoors open 6:00pm\, Event starts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nFree r
 egistration required.\nTicket policy:\nWe oversell these events to ensure 
 that the greatest number of people have an opportunity to attend. Tickets 
 guaranteed until 6:45pm or until capacity is reached. Seating first come\,
  first served.\n***\nThis event is part of our signature\nSalon Series\n\,
  where we host local and international authors\, artists and thinkers in c
 onversation about their new books and big ideas.\nThis event is also part 
 of TPL's\nBlack History and Culture Series\n.\nPresenting Sponsor of Herit
 age Programs: TD Bank Group\, through the TD Ready Commitment\n***\nInform
 ation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collect
 ed by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections
  20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event an
 d program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Registrants may be conta
 cted to fill out a brief survey following the program. Questions about how
  the library handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff 
 at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G
 8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Bram and Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto 
 Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69a5f8bd8089f23c675f5420
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Kanzashi Fabric Flower Brooch
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260512T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260512T200000
UID:69e2915f1e64afd01e69e907@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:What is Kanzashi? Kanzashi is a Japanese form of folding and s
 titching fabric to make a flower. These flowers are often used as decorati
 ve elements in various crafts and accessories. They are traditionally used
  as hair ornaments\, but can also be attached to clothing\, jewelry\, home
  decorations and even gifts. In this workshop\, participant will learn ho
 w to make their very own Kanzashi flower brooch. Hand stitching with thre
 ad and needle is required for this program.\nAll supplies will be provided
 .\nRegistration required.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal in
 formation on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under th
 e Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The informati
 on will only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and eva
 luation. Questions about how the library handles your information can be d
 irected to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Y
 onge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Sanderson\, Program room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69e2915f1e64afd01e69e907
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Google AI Essentials: In Person Learning Circle
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260428T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260428T150000
UID:69b16b746724c63d00f8192c@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Interested in learning about artificial intelligence (AI)? Let
 's learn the basics of AI in a welcoming space.\nWe will meet in person at
 \nLillian H. Smith Branch\non\nApril 7-April 28 from 1-3 pm\n.\nAI Essenti
 als Learning Circles are study groups guided by staff for people who want 
 to take the Google AI Essentials online course together in a welcoming spa
 ce. Learners meet weekly and work together through the course materials. P
 articipants are expected to commit to the duration of the course and atten
 d all learning circle sessions.\nGoogle AI Essentials is designed to help 
 people across roles and industries get essential AI skills to boost their 
 productivity. Zero experience is required.\nAt each session\, you'll discu
 ss the content of the course\, have time to catch up on the course work\, 
 and learn about related library resources.\nGoogle AI Essentials consists 
 of 5 sections and takes approximately 10 hours to complete:\nIntroduction 
 to AI - Learn about foundational concepts such as machine learning and gen
 erative AI.\nMaximize productivity with AI tools - Learn how to use genera
 tive AI tools to increase productivity in completing work tasks.\nDiscover
  the art of prompting - Learn how to write effective prompts to get better
  outputs using AI tools.\nUse AI responsibly - Learn how to lessen harmful
  biases and inaccuracies. Recognize the security risks of using AI in the 
 workplace.\nStay Ahead of the AI Curve - Learn how to stay up-to-date with
  the latest AI news.\nAccess to the course and certification are provided 
 at no cost to individuals who participate in the learning circle.\nLearner
 s who complete the course will be able to:\nearn a certificate to be share
 d on LinkedIn and social media profiles\ndownload a certificate of complet
 ion\nThe registration process is a two-step procedure. First\, participant
 s must complete their registration through this page. After this step\, li
 brary staff will follow up to request additional information in order to c
 omplete the registration.\nParticipants must have a valid Toronto Public L
 ibrary card to register.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal inf
 ormation on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the
  Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The informatio
 n will only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and eval
 uation. Questions about how the library handles your information can be di
 rected to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yo
 nge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8
LOCATION:Lillian H. Smith\, Room B/C (basement)
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69b16b746724c63d00f8192c
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
