BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Google AI Essentials: In Person Learning Circle
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260421T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260421T150000
UID:69b3562f8d5e2d093364ed90@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Curious about artificial intelligence or want to learn practic
 al AI skills for work and personal use? Join our staff-facilitated learnin
 g circle to complete the self-paced Google AI Essentials specialization.\n
 This 10-hour introductory-level specialization\, offered through Coursera\
 , is available for free to learning circle participants. It is made up of 
 five courses\, each containing four units that consist of short videos\, a
 rticles\, assignments and a short multiple-choice quiz.\nBy completing the
  specialization\,\nyou will learn:\nHow AI works\nHow to write effective p
 rompts\nHow to use generative AI tools responsibly\nHow to identify AI's p
 otential biases and avoid harm\nHow to stay up to date with AI news\nAfter
  you complete the course\, you earn a Google certificate and a badge to sh
 are with others\nLearning Circles are study groups guided by\nlibrary staf
 f for people who want to complete the Google AI Essentials specialization 
 together in a welcoming space:\nLearners meet weekly and work together thr
 ough course materials.\nParticipants are expected to commit to attending a
 ll four learning circle sessions.\nNo AI experience is required.\nHow to J
 oi\nn a Learning Circle:\nRegister once on the first session of this event
  series\, in order to attend all sessions.\nPlease sign up with a\nvalid e
 mail address\n.\nA valid TPL library card is required for online circles
 .\nAttend all four sessions at\nYork Woods Branch on Tuesdays from April 7
  to April 28\, 2026 from 1-3 pm.\nRegistration is a two-step procedure. F
 irst\, participants must complete their registration through this page. Af
 ter this step\, library staff will follow up to request additional informa
 tion in order to complete the registration.\nInformation Collection Notice
 :\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Publi
 c Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20
 (d). The information will only be used for event and program registration\
 , delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your in
 formation can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 41
 6-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:York Woods
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69b3562f8d5e2d093364ed90
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Prompt Sounds and Effects with AI Tools
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260605T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260605T200000
UID:69a202eb8089f23c675eb01c@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Musicians and producers spend hours tweaking knobs and sliders
  to get the perfect sound. But what if you could simply describe the sound
  you're looking for - "warm and fuzzy\," "sharp and metallic\," "underwate
 r echo" - and have AI create it for you?\nA new generation of AI-powered t
 ools is making this possible\, letting you build custom sounds and effects
  using plain language descriptions right inside your music software. In th
 is session\, you'll learn the vocabulary that helps AI understand what you
 're after\, then put these tools to the test to see how close they can get
  to the sound in your head. No prior music or programming experience is re
 quired.\nRegistration is required.\nThis program is part of the Innovator 
 in Residence (AI and Music Production) program\, which is designed to help
  beginner and intermediate learners build knowledge and creative skills an
 d get hands-on with current AI tools in music production and learn about t
 he history\, copyright and ethics of generative music. For more informatio
 n and a full list of programs\, visit\ntpl.ca/airesident\n.
LOCATION:Fairview\, Digital Innovation Hub\, 3rd Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69a202eb8089f23c675eb01c
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Climate Disinformation: What We Can Do About It
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260421T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260421T194500
UID:69ca8db0bf48d63c596d3967@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:What do tobacco and fossil fuels have in common\, and why does
  it matter now? How is climate change disinformation being used against Ca
 nadians\, and what can you do to push back? What is Information Sovereignt
 y and Information Integrity\, and why does it matter?\nGet the answers to 
 these questions and more with\nPhil Newell\n\, communications co-chair of 
 the\nClimate Action Against Disinformation coalition\n\, a network of over
  120 organizations around the world\, and learn about the new Global Initi
 ative on Information Integrity on Climate Change.\nThis program is present
 ed as part of Earth Month and TPL's\nOur Fragile Planet\nprogram series\, 
 supported by Friends of Toronto Public Library\, South Chapter.​\nRegist
 ration:\nThis virtual event is free to join. Once registered\, you’ll re
 ceive an email with details on how to join the session.\nThis program will
  take place on\nZoom\nand will be recorded. Registrants will receive a lin
 k to stream the recording after the program. Captions will be available.\n
 Need help with Zoom?\n-\nJoining and participating in a Zoom webinar\n-\nZ
 oom Privacy Statement\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal inform
 ation on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Pu
 blic Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information w
 ill only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluat
 ion. Questions about how the library handles your information can be direc
 ted to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge
  Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69ca8db0bf48d63c596d3967
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Classic Twists: The Turn of the Screw
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260515T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260515T130000
UID:69dd16264dacf581ff887b85@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:"The story had held us\, round the fire\, sufficiently breathl
 ess . . ."\nSince its publication in 1898\,\nThe Turn of the Screw\nhas be
 guiled generations of readers\, and inspired countless adaptations. A dece
 ptively simple story\, in which the governess of two boys at an English co
 untry house becomes convinced that her wards are haunted\, this taut gothi
 c novella challenges readers to determine the borderline of the paranormal
  and the imagination. Wrapped up in its gripping\, page-turning narrative 
 are fundamental questions about the nature of reality\, and whether the tr
 ue haunting comes from restless ghosts or from the unresolved energies of 
 memory and history.\nIn this next instalment of Toronto Public Library's s
 eries on classic literature\, host Rebecca Panovka is joined by acclaimed 
 novelist Colm Tóibín and editor Kiara Barrow to tease out the ambiguous\
 , endlessly generative threads of this supreme masterpiece.\nABOUT THIS EV
 ENT'S GUESTS:\nColm Tóibín\nwas born in Enniscorthy in 1955. He is the a
 uthor of 11 novels including\nThe Master\, Brooklyn\, The Testament of Mar
 y\, Nora Webster\, House of Names\nand\nThe Magician\n. His work has been 
 shortlisted for The Booker Prize three times\, has won the Costa Novel Awa
 rd and the IMPAC Award. He has also published two collections of stories a
 nd many works of non-fiction.\nKiara Barrow\nis cofounder and coeditor of\
 nThe Drift\nand a senior editor at Penguin Press.\nRebecca Panovka\nis a f
 ounding editor of\nThe Drift\n.\nHer essays and criticism have appeared in
 \nHarper's\, The New Yorker\, The New York Times\, Bookforum\, Dissent\, T
 he Paris Review\, The Drift\n\, and elsewhere.\n***\nImportant information
  for accessing this live virtual event:\nYou must register for a free tick
 et via Eventbrite to gain access to this event.\nThis event will be presen
 ted in a Zoom Webinar. Once registered\, you will receive reminder emails 
 from both Zoom and Eventbrite with links and instructions to access your u
 nique Zoom Webinar link to watch this event.\nAt the time of the event\, y
 ou must be signed in to Zoom or Eventbrite with the same email address tha
 t you used to book your ticket in order to access your unique event link. 
 Unique event links can only be open on one device at a time.\n***\nThis ev
 ent is made possible in part from a gift from the Ann Thoburn Fund.\n***\n
 Accessibility at Toronto Public Library\nWe are committed to making our co
 ntent accessible to as many Torontonians as possible. Please call or email
  us if you are Deaf or have a disability and would like to request accommo
 dation to participate in this program. Please let us know as far in advanc
 e as possible and we will do our best to meet your request. At least three
  weeks' notice is preferred. Phone 416-393-7099 or email accessibleservice
 s@tpl.ca
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69dd16264dacf581ff887b85
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
