BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: Searching Colours for the Stories by Marina Bu
 rke
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260401T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260430T235959
UID:69b02ade4a9fd98b7f8349ac@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout the month of April\, the works of artist Marina Bur
 ke are on display at Leaside branch in an exhibit titled Searching Colours
  for the Stories.\nMarina is a visual artist who lives and creates in Toro
 nto. She has been an active member of The Artists' Network in the East end
  of the city. She likes to experiment with art thus living in a constant 
 search for the right medium to depict the world inside and out.\n"I am con
 stantly evolving as an artist and as a person. The river of life brings th
 e feeling of flow and it is a fascinating ability to experience it. I like
  depicting people and their faces\, trying to reach out to their inner sel
 f\, to capture their spirits\, and\, in the end\, expressing my own feelin
 gs and visions of each and every model that I have."\nThis exhibit is part
  of\nTPL's Art Exhibit\nprogram. Toronto Public Library provides space for
  local artists to exhibit their work at locations across the city. These e
 xhibits aim to reflect the diverse cultural interests of the city and its 
 neighbourhoods.\nThe exhibit can be viewed during\nLeaside Branch\nopen ho
 urs. Please check the Leaside Branch page before your visit.\nFree. All ar
 e welcome.
LOCATION:Leaside\, Community Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69b02ade4a9fd98b7f8349ac
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Author Talk with Steve Burgess: The Philosophy and Practice of Liv
 ing Well on a Small Budget
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260512T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260512T193000
UID:69dbf30666c3387a9d0697c6@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Blending personal memoir\, economic philosophy\, and sharp soc
 ial observation\, Vancouver-based author\nSteve Burgess\nwill talk about h
 is new book\,\nCheapskate in Lotusland: The Philosophy and Practice of Li
 ving Well on a Small Budget\,\nand his tips on living well without ever ea
 rning too much.\nSet in Vancouver\, one of North America's most expensive 
 cities and comparable to Toronto\, the book explores frugality as a way of
  life rather than a sacrifice. From mastering grocery store bargains to qu
 estioning the relentless push of consumerism\, Burgess offers lessons in l
 iving comfortably on less—without deprivation or despair\, and that fina
 ncial security isn’t always about numbers—it’s about priorities\, pe
 rspective and knowing the value of a good pair of shoes.\nAbout The Author
 \nSteve Burgess is a writer and broadcaster whose honours include two Cana
 dian National Magazine awards. Burgess is a contributing editor of The Tye
 e and an award-winning documentary director. He is also the author of Rese
 rvations: The Pleasures and Perils of Travel (2024) which was longlisted f
 or the Leacock Medal. His stories have been featured in publications inclu
 ding Reader’s Digest\, Maclean’s\, and The Globe and Mail. He lives in
  Vancouver\, BC.\nRegistration:\nThis virtual event is free to join and wi
 ll take place on\nZoom\n. Register to receive an email with details on ho
 w to join the session.\nThis program will not be recorded.\nCaptions will 
 be available.\nNeed help with Zoom?\n-\nJoining and participating in a Zoo
 m webinar\n-\nZoom Privacy Statement\nPlace a hold or borrow from Toronto 
 Public Library:\nCheapskate in Lotusland\n(book)\nCheapskate in Lotusland\
 n(ebook)\nOther books available at TPL\nby Steve Burgess.\nInformation Col
 lection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the
  Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 
 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program
  registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library h
 andles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline
 @tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69dbf30666c3387a9d0697c6
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Between the Lines Bookclub
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260515T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260515T200000
UID:6962837f91e4576f847bd0ff@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Become a page-turner by joining the Between the Lines Book Clu
 b! Here\, we embark on a new chapter with every read\, diving into a world
  of endless possibilities. From gripping thrillers to heartwarming coming-
 of-age tales\, or a sprinkle of fantasy adventures\, there's something for
  everyone. Whether you're a die-hard fan of one genre or love mixing thing
 s up\, you'll find a place here to discuss\, share\, and connect with fell
 ow readers. Ready to explore different worlds and discover your next favor
 ite book? Join us and let?s turn the page together!\nFriday April 17:\nThe
  Seven Husbands of Evelyn Hugo by Taylor Jenkins Reid\nA reclusive Holly
 wood legend finally tells her story—on her own terms. The Seven Husbands
  of Evelyn Hugo pulls readers into the dazzling and ruthless world of old 
 Hollywood\, where fame comes at a price and love is rarely simple. As Evel
 yn recounts her rise to stardom and the truth behind her seven marriages\,
  long-buried secrets begin to surface. It’s a story of ambition\, identi
 ty\, sacrifice\, and a love that defies expectations—perfect for a book 
 club ready to discuss glamour\, heartbreak\, and what it really means to l
 ive authentically.\nIf you need help getting a copy of the book or have an
 y questions\, please contact us at sastaff@tpl.ca or 416-393-7653. Copies 
 of the monthly titles are available at the circulation desk.
LOCATION:Sanderson\, Main Floor Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6962837f91e4576f847bd0ff
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
