BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Author Talk: Zoe S. Roy
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260506T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260506T153000
UID:6995044bb48451fc3c035dbc@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Zoë S. Roy presents and discusses the theme of "Noodles" her 
 short story from\nButterfly Tears\n\, a collection of her stories.\n“No
 odles” tells the story of a Chinese immigrant woman adjusting to life in
  Toronto and explores her journey through the emotional and physical chall
 enges of adapting to a new language and culture.\nDrop-in
LOCATION:Runnymede\, Program room\, 2nd floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6995044bb48451fc3c035dbc
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Life and Art of Mashel Teitelbaum
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260514T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260514T143000
UID:69c43e66491b809c6f22e5d5@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:One of Canada’s most talented and influential artistic outsi
 ders\, and the son of Jewish immigrants\, Mashel Teitelbaum grew up during
  the Great Depression\, World War II\, and the Holocaust\, before honing h
 is voice under the likes of Clyfford Still\, Ad Reinhardt\, and Max Beckma
 nn. Teitelbaum created a body of work that remains arresting to this day. 
 From landscapes to portraits\, expressionistic abstraction to conceptual i
 nstallations\, his art was characterized by self-expression and a desire t
 o speak truth to power\, often placing him in conflict with the establishm
 ent. Join curator Andrew Kear for an illustrated presentation\, followed 
 by book sales and signings.\nPresented in partnership with Toronto Public
  Library as part of its Jewish Heritage Series\, and the Canadian Jewish L
 iterary Awards.\nJoin us in the Al Green Theatre\, on Zoom simulcast\, or 
 register to receive the recording.\nAbout this event’s guests:\nAndrew K
 ear\nis Senior Curator and Head of Collections\, Exhibitions\, and Program
 s at Museum London. He was formerly the Head of Collections and Exhibitio
 ns and Curator of Canadian Art at the Winnipeg Art Gallery. Kear began hi
 s career at the Tom Thomson Art Gallery in Owen Sound\, and has taught Can
 adian Art at the University of Winnipeg. He has also published widely\, in
 cluding the 2017 Art Canada Institute publication of\nWilliam Kurelek (192
 7-1977)\n.\nHis curatorial efforts include\nWilliam Kurelek: The Messenger
 \n(2011)\n\,\nStorm and Spirit: The Eckhardt-Gramatté Collection of Germa
 n Expressionist Art\n(2013)\,\nDali Up Close\n(2014)\,\nKarel Funk\n(2016)
 \,\nThe 80s Image\n(2018) and\nRealisms: Canadian Art\, 1850 to the Presen
 t\n(2020). Andrew’s writing has been published in\nCanadian Art\n\,\nBor
 der Crossings\n\, and\nSculpture\n.\n***\nThis event is part of our signat
 ure\nSalon Series\n\, where we host local and international authors\, arti
 sts and thinkers in conversation about their new books and big ideas.\nIt 
 is also part of our programming series\,\nJewish Heritage\n\,\ncreated in 
 collaboration with\nMiles Nadal - Jewish Community Centre\n. Join us in ce
 lebrating the diversity that strengthens our community and makes our city 
 a more vibrant and inclusive place for all.\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocati
 on:\nAl Green Theatre\, Miles Nadal Jewish Community Centre\, 750 Spadina 
 Ave.\nTime:\nDoors open 12:30pm\, Event begins 1:00pm\nTickets:\nFree regi
 stration required.\nTicket policy:\nWe oversell these events to ensure tha
 t the greatest number of people have an opportunity to attend. Tickets gua
 ranteed until 6:45pm or until capacity is reached. Seating first come\, fi
 rst served.\n***\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information
  on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public 
 Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will o
 nly be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. 
 Registrants may be contacted to fill out a brief survey following the prog
 ram. Questions about how the library handles your information can be direc
 ted to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge St
 reet\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69c43e66491b809c6f22e5d5
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Modern Fairy Tales on Film: Pan's Labyrinth
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260424T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260424T130000
UID:69c444e01e64afd01e64b449@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:In this continuation of Toronto Public Library's virtual serie
 s on Modern Fairy Tales on Film\, host Adam Nayman explores\nPan's Labyrin
 th\n\, the dark fantasy of Guillermo Del Toro\, which premiered to critica
 l acclaim and popular success in 2006. Set against the nightmarish backdro
 p of Francoist Spain\, the film centres upon the adventurous Ofelia\, a ch
 ild who discovers the titular overgrown labyrinth\, and strikes up a begui
 ling relationship with a mysterious faun. The story is a springboard for s
 ome of Del Toro's most audacious flights of fancy\, combining animatronics
  with CGI to create a singular visual masterpiece. Along with critic Soray
 a Sebghati and author Silvia Moreno-Garcia\, Nayman will navigate the twis
 ted byways of this maze-like masterpiece\, all the way to the heart of its
  mythical allure.\nABOUT THIS EVENT'S GUESTS:\nSoraya Sebghati\nis\nan L.A
 .-born-and-raised film critic\nand the front woman of the local band Night
  Talks. Her writing on film can be found on her\nSubstack\n.\nSilvia Moren
 o-Garcia\nis the author of\nThe Bewitching\n\,\nThe Seventh Veil of Salome
 \, The Daughter of Doctor Moreau\n\,\nMexican Gothic\n\, and many other bo
 oks. She has won the Locus\, British Fantasy and World Fantasy awards.\nAd
 am Nayman\nis a critic\, teacher\, and author\, whose books include critic
 al surveys of the Coen Brothers\, Paul Thomas Anderson\, and most recently
 \, in\nMind Games\n\, the filmmaker David Fincher. He is a regular contrib
 utor to\nThe Ringer\n\, a lecturer at the University of Toronto's Cinema S
 tudies Institute\, and he has written on film for\nThe New Yorker\nand\nTh
 e New York Times\n.\n***\nImportant information for accessing this live vi
 rtual event:\nYou must register for a free ticket via Eventbrite to gain a
 ccess to this event.\nThis event will be presented in a Zoom Webinar. Once
  registered\, you will receive reminder emails from both Zoom and Eventbri
 te with links and instructions to access your unique Zoom Webinar link to 
 watch this event.\nAt the time of the event\, you must be signed in to Zoo
 m or Eventbrite with the same email address that you used to book your tic
 ket in order to access your unique event link. Unique event links can only
  be open on one device at a time.\n***\nAccessibility at Toronto Public Li
 brary\nWe are committed to making our content accessible to as many Toront
 onians as possible. Please call or email us if you are Deaf or have a disa
 bility and would like to request accommodation to participate in this prog
 ram. Please let us know as far in advance as possible and we will do our b
 est to meet your request. At least three weeks' notice is preferred. Phone
  416-393-7099 or email accessibleservices@tpl.ca
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69c444e01e64afd01e64b449
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Mix and Master Music with AI Tools
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260428T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260428T200000
UID:69a200a94dacf581ff7e66c5@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:You've written a great song\, but why doesn't it sound like th
 e music you hear on the radio or your favourite streaming playlist? The an
 swer usually comes down to mixing and mastering\, the final stages of musi
 c production that give a track its polish and professional sound.\nThese w
 ere actually the first areas of music production where AI tools proved the
 mselves\, because much of the process involves technical decisions that ca
 n be measured and optimized. In this session\, you'll learn what mixing an
 d mastering are\, why they matter\, and how AI tools can help you achieve 
 professional-quality results in a fraction of the time. No prior music or
  programming experience is required.\nThis program is part of the Innovato
 r in Residence (AI and Music Production) program\, which is designed to he
 lp beginner and intermediate learners build knowledge and creative skills 
 and get hands-on with current AI tools in music production and learn about
  the history\, copyright and ethics of generative music. For more informat
 ion and a full list of programs\, visit tpl.ca/airesident.
LOCATION:Fairview\, Digital Innovation Hub\, 3rd Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69a200a94dacf581ff7e66c5
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Worlds of Music: Write AI Prompts for Different Music Styles
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260502T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260502T160000
UID:69a2013e1e64afd01e5e90a9@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Music comes in an incredible variety of styles from cultures a
 round the world\, but most AI music tools have been trained primarily on W
 estern music traditions. So what happens when you want to create something
  different?\nIn this session\, you'll explore the musical building blocks 
 that define different styles from around the globe\, including scales\, rh
 ythms\, and structures that may be unfamiliar to Western ears. Then you'll
  experiment with prompting techniques to see how far you can push AI tools
  beyond their comfort zone. Some experiments will succeed\, some won't\, a
 nd both outcomes will teach you something valuable about the possibilities
  and limitations of today's AI music technology. No prior music or progra
 mming experience is required. Participants are encouraged to bring example
 s of music they’d like to explore.\nRegistration is required.\nThis prog
 ram is part of the Innovator in Residence (AI and Music Production) progra
 m\, which is designed to help beginner and intermediate learners build kno
 wledge and creative skills and get hands-on with current AI tools in music
  production and learn about the history\, copyright and ethics of generati
 ve music. For more information and a full list of programs\, visit\ntpl.ca
 /airesident\n.
LOCATION:Fairview\, Digital Innovation Hub\, 3rd Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69a2013e1e64afd01e5e90a9
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Nourish Your Best Weight
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260428T173000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260428T190000
UID:69c5506db6c4ac1fedc84705@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Learn how to build a healthier relationship with food\, unders
 tand weight bias\, and support long-term health in this interactive sessio
 n.\nWeight is often at the centre of conversations about health\, but buil
 ding a sustainable and supportive relationship with food and your body goe
 s far beyond the number on the scale. This session offers a thoughtful\, e
 vidence-based approach to nutrition\, focusing on long-term habits\, self-
 awareness\, and overall well-being.\nWhat to Expect:\nUnderstanding Weight
  Bias:\nLearn how weight bias can impact health\, self-care\, and the way 
 we approach nutrition.\nYour Relationship with Food:\nReflect on your pers
 onal relationship with food and how it shapes your eating habits.\nRethink
 ing Health and Wellness:\nExplore a broader perspective on health that goe
 s beyond the number on the scale.\nBuilding Sustainable Habits:\nLearn pra
 ctical strategies to support long-term nourishment and well-being\, rather
  than quick fixes.\nAbout the Speaker\nThis interactive session will be le
 d by Catherine Cai\, Registered Dietitian and Certified Diabetes Educator 
 at Crosstown Family Health Team\, who will provide practical information a
 nd guidance to help you better understand your relationship with food and 
 how to support your health.\nThis program is presented by Toronto Public L
 ibrary in collaboration with the West Toronto Ontario Health Team (OHT)\, 
 a network of over 40 local health and social service providers working tog
 ether to improve the health and well-being of communities in West Toronto.
 \nYour Health Matters\nThis program is part of Toronto Public Library’s\
 nYour Health Matters\ninitiative\, which offers trusted health programs an
 d resources to help people make informed decisions about their health. Lea
 rn more by exploring the\nYour Health Matters web page\n.\nReserve Your Sp
 ot Today!\nThis virtual event is free to join but registration is required
 .\nPlease register on Eventbrite\n.\nOnce registered\, you’ll receive an
  email with details on how to join the session.\nWe can’t wait to see yo
 u there!
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69c5506db6c4ac1fedc84705
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:DM Poetry Circle Presents
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260429T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260429T200000
UID:69a05d4fcbf1533ca5387c04@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:April is Poetry Month.\nJoin members of the Don Mills Poetry C
 ircle as they present authentic works created over the past year!\nThe Don
  Mills Poetry Circle started in January 2017\, growing out of an "Arts in 
 the Library" program. The group meets twice a month. At the end of each me
 eting\, the members take turns giving a prompt to write on for the next me
 eting and it is always interesting to hear the different takes the poets t
 ake on a single prompt.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal info
 rmation on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the 
 Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information
  will only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evalu
 ation. Questions about how the library handles your information can be dir
 ected to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yon
 ge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Don Mills\, Auditorium
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69a05d4fcbf1533ca5387c04
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: Trees by Bernardo Cioppa
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260402T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260429T235959
UID:69c56480491b809c6f2310ed@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout the month of April\, works by artist Bernardo Ciop
 pa are on display at Northern District Library branch in an exhibit title
 d Trees.\nBernardo Cioppa began photography at age 10 with a Kodak Brownie
  camera. He mainly focuses on street photography but also creates landscap
 es. Through the York Arts Guild\, he exhibits his work around Toronto and 
 is currently showing at the Tranzac Club. He has sold several photos\, par
 ticipated in various exhibitions\, and won second place in Visual Arts Mis
 sissauga’s 2025 “Why Do You Create?” juried exhibition\n"I’m inspi
 red by people's incidents of being or doing intriguing things. As such\, s
 treet photography speaks to me. My approach is more inspirational rather t
 han technical. I'm creating a one frame movie. The photo will tell a story
  or prompt questions about the scene or behaviour of people and capture th
 at poignant moment before forever gone. You need to be decisive\, taking i
 nto consideration composition\, direction of light\, and subject matter\, 
 and mixed with some luck\, hopefully catch the moment."\nThis exhibit is p
 art of TPL's\nArt Exhibits Space\nprogram. Toronto Public Library provides
  space for local artists to exhibit their work at locations across the cit
 y. These exhibits aim to reflect the diverse cultural interests of the cit
 y and its neighbourhoods.\nThe exhibit can be viewed during\nNorthern Dist
 rict\nLibrary open hours.\nFree. All are welcome.\nImage credit: Bernardo
  Cioppa\; photography\; Today's Favourite
LOCATION:Northern District\, 2nd floor Gallery space
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69c56480491b809c6f2310ed
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Navigating Cancer Treatment Decisions with Confidence
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260423T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260423T200000
UID:69c580d19057005337d08aa6@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Making decisions about cancer treatment can feel overwhelming\
 , whether facing a new diagnosis or managing recurrent disease. This sessi
 on offers clear\, supportive information to help individuals and families 
 prepare for conversations with their care team and approach treatment deci
 sions with greater confidence and clarity.\nWhat to Expect:\nUnderstanding
  Your Care Options:\nLearn about factors that may influence your treatment
  decisions and how to approach them.\nFinding Reliable Information:\nBecom
 e more familiar with your diagnosis and available treatment options\, incl
 uding where to access trusted information.\nBuilding Your Support System:\
 nIdentify people and services that can support you throughout your care jo
 urney.\nPreparing for Appointments:\nLearn how to prepare helpful question
 s so you can communicate your concerns and preferences effectively.\nSuppo
 rting Caregivers:\nExplore the important role caregivers play in treatment
  decision-making and how they can offer meaningful support.\nFocusing on W
 hat Matters Most:\nReflect on your values and priorities when evaluating d
 ifferent treatment options.\nAbout the Speaker\nThis session will be led b
 y Lisa Malinowski Kamran (she/her)\, Registered Social Worker (MSW\, RSW) 
 and Membership and Outreach Coordinator at Gilda’s Toronto. Drawing on h
 er experience in social work\, mindfulness\, and community-based health in
 itiatives\, she will share practical tools and guidance to support informe
 d and compassionate decision-making during cancer care.\nThis program is p
 resented by Toronto Public Library in collaboration with the West Toronto 
 Ontario Health Team (OHT)\, a network of over 40 local health and social s
 ervice providers working together to improve the health and well-being of 
 communities in West Toronto.\nYour Health Matters\nThis program is part of
  Toronto Public Library’s\nYour Health Matters\ninitiative\, which offer
 s trusted health programs and resources to help people make informed decis
 ions about their health. Learn more by exploring the\nYour Health Matters 
 web page\n.\nReserve Your Spot Today!\nThis virtual event is free to join 
 but registration is required.\nPlease register on Eventbrite\n.\nOnce regi
 stered\, you’ll receive an email with details on how to join the session
 .\nWe can’t wait to see you there!
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69c580d19057005337d08aa6
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Writers Collective of Canada (WCC): Community Writing Workshop
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260502T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260502T113000
UID:69c58197b48451fc3c0b7524@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:When we write with others\, we discover ourselves\n.\nWCC work
 shops offer a brave creative space where all are welcomed to write and exp
 lore their story in a space free of critique.\nThe workshops are led by tr
 ained volunteer facilitators who also act as participants. The group write
 s in response to prompts\, is invited to share their first draft writing a
 nd offer affirming feedback to one another.\nWhile the focus is on the pro
 cess of writing rather than a finished product - the stories that emerge i
 n WCC workshops are magnificent. In sharing and listening to one another's
  stories\, participants find connection with self and others.\nNo experien
 ce required. Beginners are welcome. All materials are provided.\nInformati
 on Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected 
 by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20
 (a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and p
 rogram registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the lib
 rary handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nansw
 erline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Gerrard/Ashdale\, 2nd Floor Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69c58197b48451fc3c0b7524
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: Works by Andrew Johnston
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260402T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260429T235959
UID:69c585fa4dacf581ff8492fb@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout April\, works by artist Andrew Johnston are on disp
 lay at Mimico Centennial branch.\nAndrew Johnston is largely self-taught\,
  and enjoys being surrounded by nature and in other interactive milieu.\n"
 I make fine art. My voyage of self-discovery has brought me this far. I ha
 ve the desire to create more."\nThis exhibit is part of TPL's\nArt Exhibit
 s Space\nprogram. Toronto Public Library provides space for local artists 
 to exhibit their work at locations across the city. These exhibits aim to 
 reflect the diverse cultural interests of the city and its neighbourhoods.
 \nThe exhibit can be viewed during\nMimico Centennial\nbranch open hours.\
 nFree. All are welcome.\nImage credit: Andrew Johnston\; acrylic\; white 
 irises
LOCATION:Mimico Centennial\, Mezzanine
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69c585fa4dacf581ff8492fb
