BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Journalism Series with David Silverberg: Adding vivid details to y
 our non-fiction work (workshop)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260526T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260526T193000
UID:69c81fe8b48451fc3c0bd8f4@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:This workshop is presented by the North York Central Library's
  2026 Writer in Residence David Silverberg\, whose focus is on Journalism.
 \nFrom hard news articles to creative non-fiction\, specifics matter. Conc
 rete descriptions of your article’s location and its main interviewees w
 ill help immerse readers in your story. In this workshop\, you’ll take p
 art in several exercises designed to bring colour and sensory details to y
 our writing. You’ll also learn from examples written by some of the most
  iconic journalists in the world such as Joan Didion and Pierre Berton.\nT
 his lecture will be followed by Q&A\nLearn more about TPL's\nWriter in Res
 idence\nprogram\, including upcoming programs and one-on-one consultation 
 sessions.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on thi
 s form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Librari
 es Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be 
 used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questio
 ns about how the library handles your information can be directed to Answe
 rline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, To
 ronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, Room 101\, 1st floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69c81fe8b48451fc3c0bd8f4
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Journalism Series with David Silverberg: How to write a feature ar
 ticle
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260603T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260603T193000
UID:69c81fe8b48451fc3c0bd8f5@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:This lecture is presented by the North York Central Library's 
 2026 Writer in Residence David Silverberg\, whose focus is on Journalism.\
 nWriting for news networks requires blending research\, quotes and strong 
 writing but how do these building blocks flow together seamlessly? In this
  presentation on how to structure a feature article\, learn how to arrange
  all those elements into an engaging piece of journalism. Perfect for anyo
 ne who wants to understand the blueprint of an article and writers looking
  to develop their own features.\nThis program will be followed by Q&A\nLea
 rn more about TPL's\nWriter in Residence\nprogram\, including upcoming pro
 grams and one-on-one consultation sessions.\nInformation Collection Notice
 :\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Publi
 c Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20
 (d). The information will only be used for event and program registration\
 , delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your in
 formation can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 41
 6-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, Room 101\, 1st floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69c81fe8b48451fc3c0bd8f5
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Journalism Series with David Silverberg: How to write an opinion a
 rticle (workshop)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260609T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260609T193000
UID:69c81fe8b48451fc3c0bd8f6@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:This workshop is presented by the North York Central Library's
  2026 Writer in Residence David Silverberg\, whose focus is on Journalism.
 \nStructured differently than feature news articles\, opinion journalism a
 sks for unique writing skills. You’ll find out how this type of article 
 lets you write on your own experiences and outlooks on work\, relationship
 s\, headline news\, culture and much more. But what’s the right way to m
 ake your argument to readers? Also\, what can you learn from the most enga
 ging opinion writers in Canada? In this workshop\, be prepared to write yo
 ur own opinion article.\nThis program will be followed by Q&A\nLearn more 
 about TPL's\nWriter in Residence\nprogram\, including upcoming programs an
 d one-on-one consultation sessions.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe p
 ersonal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Librar
 y under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The
  information will only be used for event and program registration\, delive
 ry and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your informatio
 n can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-59
 81\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
